A||N||14||Verticalspeed||Do you like your poly bushings?||||6||
Z||000000||Verticalspeed||01-09-2001||06:10 PM||||Wondering if that has made a significant difference that a stock new bushings replacement wouldn't.||||reg||6
Z||000001||Oreif||01-09-2001||06:17 PM||||Yes it has made a great improvement over rubber bushings. Yes I like them.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||01-09-2001||07:11 PM||||Yes, rides like a rock. I took them out and learned how to replace the rubber bushings. In the end you will need to watch the poly as much as the rubber for compression.<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||01-09-2001||08:09 PM||||I love my poly bushings. The first month they were perfect, then the second month they would squeak on cold mornings, and ever since then they have been silent. I love 'em and have no plans of ever going back to rubber.||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||01-09-2001||08:10 PM||||i have enjoyed mine, even with the problem i have... they are a much better improvement over stock!||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfiero||01-09-2001||08:39 PM||||I have found that putting poly on the rear and keeping the rubber up front works well with lowered springs (at least on the '88 Fiero).<P>I put some poly bushings on the front control arms and installed the lowered springs on my '88 GT (now sold).  I found that it rode very harsh and handled worse than before.<P>I think the combination of the poly bushings and the lowered springs is too stiff, so from now on I will keep the rubber up front.<BR> <BR>However, if you keeping the factory springs then by all means use the poly on all 4 corners.  It eally tghtens up an otherwise sloppy suspension.||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||01-09-2001||09:54 PM||||I'm now using poly in the rear only(cradle,and control arms),since it was the torque that was squishing things around,front works just fine with rubber so far,and I'm running RE-71s(205-16/225-16)all around.I had some rear bushings in my old car that I made from UHMW plastic,and worked much better(less bind,better ride),didn't have a lathe this time though.||||reg||1
Z||000007||v8vinnie||01-09-2001||10:14 PM||||yea me too, im going to keep the stock rubber bushings in front with the pro springs, and i just finished the poly bushings in cradle and lower control arms. i probably need as much control with that 345hp v8 back there, going to finish up the front end this weekend, took it for a quick run around the block and i can already feel the tighness,but i got to get the 4 wheel alignment doon before i really kill the tires.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Archie||01-09-2001||10:57 PM||||By Golly, I like my bushings of Poly.<P>I'm a poet & don't know it.<P>Archie<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Verticalspeed||01-10-2001||01:18 PM||||Does the ride become "more harsh"?<BR>Does the car feel more like it's on rails?<BR>Does the car "bounce less" on crappy roads?<P>All of the above?<P>Is there a web page with pictures showing the bushing removal/installation process? <P>O.K. the majority convinces me that poly is the way to go! I will start with the rear low control arms and cradle bushings.<BR>I will order mine from the fiero store.||||reg||5
Z||000010||1FST2M6||01-10-2001||01:39 PM||||the car will only be harsh if you have Konis running REALLY tight or springs that are too high a load rating.  Poly will make the car stop squeeking inside and make it feel new. it'll take A-LOT of play out of the steering and it'll ditch the "wiggle" some get from the arm's moving in and changin' the toe-in.  You'll like them...<P>Prothane part number 7-304($20), and 7-207($33) also the cradle mounts 7-502 ($48)||||reg||1
Z||000011||2birds||01-10-2001||06:17 PM||||I've had poly all around for 6 years, with Suspension Techniques springs and a rear anti-sway bar.  The rear tires wear evenly now (I think the poly minimizes the rear camber change), and it handles much better in turns, but it follows every single groove in the road.  75 mph is a handful around here.  Cross winds really make it crazy.  Since cold (&lt;50 degree) weather makes it worse, I suspect it's mostly the bushings not spinning easily.  It feels like the whole car is unsprung weight now.  <BR>I'm going to try to install some grease fittings on each bushing, with grooves in each bushing's outer face for the grease.  I think the combination of the linear rate springs and the stiff bushings has forced the tire to absorb almost all of the suspension action, instead of the springs.  <P>I also have noticed flaking and  spiderwebbing in my paint at points around the sunroof corners, so maybe the body is flexing more too. <P>I think if I was doing it again, I'd do just the rear and the cradle, no mater what springs I used.  Spring choice may affect it, but I put my stock springs back in for 6 months and didn't really notice any improvement, using my highly calibrated backside meter.<P>My $1.02||||reg||1
Z||000012||2birds||01-10-2001||06:21 PM||||Those who have used them, do you ever re-torque your control arm mounting bolts?  When you installed them, what torque did you use?  I torqued mine to the stock specs in the Haynes manual, for lack of any other guidance, and I checked them about two years ago, and they didn't appear to have loosened at all.||||reg||1
Z||000013||lowCG||01-10-2001||08:22 PM||||When assembling,I try and measure all the parts(steel sleeves) to see how much squish will occur,especially with aftermarket poly.Should only be .010-.020,or even just 0.<BR>Once the bolts tighten onto the sleeves,they should stay tight,right?||||reg||1
Z||000014||2birds||01-11-2001||09:17 PM||||I think that makes sense, if I understand you correctly.  I'm concerned about the poly wearing, since it is supposed to rotate in the cup or around the insert tube.  Even the grease I got with mine can't be effective forever.  The rubber bushings just twist from what I understand.||||reg||1
